# Gates core rules

Gates is a production ERP (gates-web: Next.js 15 + TanStack Query; gates-backend: Express route -> service -> Prisma on MySQL; BullMQ + Redis; Railway). Changes must keep it working.

- Preserve existing business behavior, inputs, outputs, permissions and screens unless the task explicitly changes them.
- Make the smallest change that solves the task. No broad rewrites, no drive-by refactors.
- Read the existing implementation (route, service, Prisma model, page) before editing.
- Reuse existing Gates patterns and helpers before creating new abstractions. Do not add repositories, microservices, another ORM, another database or another state library unless the task explicitly approves it.
- Accounting and inventory correctness outranks code cleanliness.
- Database correctness never depends only on frontend behavior; assume every mutation can run twice at the same time.
- Measure before large optimizations.

## Non-trivial work

Before coding, briefly identify: affected pages, endpoints, services, Prisma models, source of truth, canonical pattern, transaction boundary, concurrency, tenant scoping, permissions, query growth / pagination, caches, tests. For posting, reversal, numbering, balances, stock, cost, tenancy or schema changes, write a plan first.

After coding, report: files changed, intended behavior changes, schema / API changes, queries introduced, transaction and concurrency handling, tests and typecheck run, remaining risks, and how accounting / inventory / tenant correctness is preserved.

Trivial text, label or styling changes need none of this.

